<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Darial Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Darial Gorge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Darial Pass is a gorge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek River</a> through the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a>, on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Since antiquity it has been the main route across the mountains, guarded by fortifications known to classical writers as the Caspian or Alan Gates. The Georgian Military Road, built by Russia in the 19th century, runs through it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darial-pass.jpg" alt="Darial Pass" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darial_Gorge,_Terek_River,_Georgia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Darial Gorge is a gorge of the River Terek on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, at the east base of Mount Kazbek and south of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladikavkaz">Vladikavkaz</a>. It is about 13 kilometres long, and its steep granite walls reach 1,800 metres in places. The Georgian Military Road runs through it.</p><p>The pass was historically one of only two crossings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> range, the other being the Derbent Pass, and it has been fortified since at least 150 BCE. Writers of late antiquity imagined it as a boundary between the known world and the unknown land of the barbarians. It is the main border crossing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the Persian Dar-i Alān, meaning Gate of the Alans, who held the lands north of the pass in the first centuries CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> both fortified it, and the fortification was known variously as the Iberian Gates or the Caucasian Gates. Classical writers often mistakenly called it the Caspian Gates. Strabo calls it Porta Caucasica and Porta Cumana, Ptolemy calls it Fortes Sarmatica, and the Georgian annals call it Darialani. Josephus wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> built iron gates at an unspecified pass that some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek authors identified with Darial.</p><p>The Sassanid Empire took the pass in 252–253 when it conquered and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. In 628 control passed to the Western Turkic Kaganate under a treaty between Tong Yabgu Kagan and Iberia, which also established free trade, and in 644 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. It was later held by the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and was a point of contention between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Kabardians occupied it in 1597, and it was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the period of its annexation of the Kingdom of Georgia, 1801–1830. It remained a strategic Russian outpost until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Darial Gorge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Caucasus the Alans controlled the passes, above all the <b>Darial Gorge</b>, which gave them leverage over the empires to the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rise drew on the agricultural wealth of the Mtkvari valley and on control of routes through the Caucasus, including the <b>Darial Pass</b>, which classical writers called the Caucasian Gates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The upper Aragvi valley lay on the route that later became the Georgian Military Road through the <b>Darial Pass</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-aragvi">Duchy of Aragvi</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings ruled a land of fortified towns and hill forts, controlling the <b>Darial Pass</b> and trade with the Black Sea and the steppe.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-alania">Kingdom of Alania</a></li></ul></section>
